Dominik Zajkowski: That limit can also be loosened over time.

171
00:21:35.860 --> 00:21:42.540
Dominik Zajkowski: Final note on this design… is that…

172
00:21:44.220 --> 00:21:54.979
Dominik Zajkowski: one of the features that is useful in Ethereum is the ability to index data. Now, in a privacy-preserving chain, index data doesn't really…

173
00:21:56.060 --> 00:21:59.900
Dominik Zajkowski: Mean much, but there's still some usefulness.

174
00:22:00.040 --> 00:22:02.230
Dominik Zajkowski: That can be built around it.

175
00:22:02.380 --> 00:22:08.740
Dominik Zajkowski: So the proposal is to allow for a second parameter in the log expression.

176
00:22:09.260 --> 00:22:13.809
Dominik Zajkowski: Which denotes which fields from the

177
00:22:14.170 --> 00:22:17.389
Dominik Zajkowski: Logged out structure should be indexed.

178
00:22:17.960 --> 00:22:21.970
Dominik Zajkowski: This is a hint for downstream consumers.

179
00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:36.059
Dominik Zajkowski: From a… usage perspective. This then translates into the ability to say, I want to filter…

180
00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:40.780
Dominik Zajkowski: Events coming from a particular address of a particular type.

181
00:22:41.230 --> 00:22:46.810
Dominik Zajkowski: with a particular prefix. So let's say the first two bytes, or the first one byte.

182
00:22:48.470 --> 00:22:54.810
Dominik Zajkowski: Now, the usefulness of this… From the perspective of…

183
00:22:55.000 --> 00:23:02.580
Dominik Zajkowski: filtering, Monero proposed a very similar thing, they just added an additional byte to their keys.

184
00:23:03.820 --> 00:23:13.360
Dominik Zajkowski: And it reduced the scan time by 50-70% of time, which seems like a nice improvement.

185
00:23:13.680 --> 00:23:20.819
Dominik Zajkowski: So, it seems like a useful feature to have. Now, it's not one-to-one Ethereum indexing.

186
00:23:21.950 --> 00:23:27.579
Dominik Zajkowski: But… best approximation that we could come up with… come up with.

187
00:23:28.340 --> 00:23:41.810
Dominik Zajkowski: I won't bore you with more details, but I'm happy to answer questions and clarify. The intended next step is to follow the COIP process and put all the relevant changes to Compact.

188
00:23:41.970 --> 00:23:43.119
Dominik Zajkowski: How's a cloip?

189
00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:45.830
Dominik Zajkowski: Sure, Kevin.

190
00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:48.699
Dominik Zajkowski: And, sorry, and Parisa.

191
00:23:49.260 --> 00:23:50.010
Dominik Zajkowski: Next.

192
00:23:50.230 --> 00:23:52.140
Kent Dybvig: I think Grisa had her hand up for a while.

193
00:23:52.580 --> 00:23:53.319
Dominik Zajkowski: Oh, sorry.

194
00:23:54.550 --> 00:24:06.530
Parisa Ataei: Yeah, I was… I'm not so sure if I understand what you mean by they should already be disclosed. Do you mean they should… so if I want to log,

195
00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:26.570
Parisa Ataei: if I want to log a ledger field, and let's say, do some operation with it to… let's just for simple… let's say, I'm adding, a witness to it. So if I say log, my, my public…

196
00:24:26.570 --> 00:24:30.579
Parisa Ataei: Ledger plus disclose my witness.

197
00:24:30.700 --> 00:24:32.509
Parisa Ataei: Should that be acceptable?

198
00:24:33.130 --> 00:24:36.460
Dominik Zajkowski: If you say disclose witness first, sure.

199
00:24:37.250 --> 00:24:42.620
Dominik Zajkowski: My intended meaning was the data has already been disclosed.

200
00:24:42.870 --> 00:24:43.860
Dominik Zajkowski: disclosed.

201
00:24:43.960 --> 00:24:47.220
Dominik Zajkowski: As in, tagged, disclosed.

202
00:24:47.630 --> 00:24:52.289
Kent Dybvig: Okay. I think that's… the terminology's a bit confusing, because…

203
00:24:52.540 --> 00:25:05.540
Kent Dybvig: It makes it sound like we're not disclosing anything that's not already being disclosed by storing in a ledger, or returning it from a cross-contract call, or something like that.

204
00:25:05.820 --> 00:25:11.590
Kent Dybvig: So, we should probably work on the wording of that to say that, you know, this is a disclosure.

205
00:25:11.910 --> 00:25:15.389
Kent Dybvig: And therefore must be explicitly declared as such.

206
00:25:16.310 --> 00:25:27.060
Dominik Zajkowski: So, the idea is that the log expression should not accept anything that wasn't already… function called disclosedOn.

207
00:25:28.120 --> 00:25:28.970
Dominik Zajkowski: Does that make sense?

208
00:25:29.760 --> 00:25:41.929
Kent Dybvig: Yeah, it's not a function call, it's just a declaration that tells the compiler that it's okay to disclose it. And so what we're saying is that it must be explicitly declared disclosed, if it's… if it's…

209
00:25:42.110 --> 00:25:43.120
Kent Dybvig: Private data.

210
00:25:43.470 --> 00:25:49.790
Dominik Zajkowski: Alternatively, the log says this hasn't been marked for disclosure, tagged for disclosure.

211
00:25:50.470 --> 00:25:54.490
Kevin Millikin: It's exactly the same as writing to a ledger field.

212
00:25:56.650 --> 00:25:58.779
Kevin Millikin: The sub-expression needs to be.

213
00:25:59.300 --> 00:25:59.840
Kent Dybvig: Right.

214
00:26:00.330 --> 00:26:01.140
Kent Dybvig: Yeah.

215
00:26:01.420 --> 00:26:06.189
Kent Dybvig: So, now, I have a question. You said… That indexed…

216
00:26:06.550 --> 00:26:12.629
Kent Dybvig: Data is not as useful in the privacy setting, but we're talking about things that are already disclosed,

217
00:26:13.770 --> 00:26:20.379
Kent Dybvig: Or maybe they're even already in the ledger about… already being stored to the ledger.

218
00:26:20.580 --> 00:26:25.429
Kent Dybvig: Why isn't it just as useful in our context as in any other context?

219
00:26:25.660 --> 00:26:39.809
Dominik Zajkowski: the challenge is that querying for data is also useful information for one that wants to observe and potentially breach privacy. So, if I'm looking for a particular value.

220
00:26:41.230 --> 00:26:47.379
Dominik Zajkowski: up to the moment that I ask for it, it's just one value out of many, there is no easy way to connect that

221
00:26:47.730 --> 00:26:49.670
Dominik Zajkowski: Data point to me.

222
00:26:50.070 --> 00:26:51.989
Dominik Zajkowski: Looking for it on the ledger.

223
00:26:52.310 --> 00:27:02.439
Dominik Zajkowski: So, if I allow myself to ask the index about a particular value, full value, now it's obvious that I'm interested in that particular field.

224
00:27:03.360 --> 00:27:12.719
Dominik Zajkowski: indexing in this… from this perspective, becomes challenging, because querying it is challenging. Therefore.

225
00:27:13.140 --> 00:27:17.309
Dominik Zajkowski: Allowing for a limited prefix.

226
00:27:18.100 --> 00:27:22.860
Dominik Zajkowski: Like, for example, the first byte, results in…

227
00:27:23.190 --> 00:27:29.180
Dominik Zajkowski: Data that still has to be refined on my side, but it… somewhat shields

228
00:27:29.830 --> 00:27:33.360
Dominik Zajkowski: This piece of information, which particular Okay.

229
00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:33.790
Kent Dybvig: Yeah.

230
00:27:33.790 --> 00:27:34.190
Dominik Zajkowski: data point.

231
00:27:34.190 --> 00:27:37.310
Kent Dybvig: I see what… I see what you're saying. This is basically, we can…

232
00:27:37.570 --> 00:27:47.820
Kent Dybvig: we're limiting the pool of people, who are interested in a particular piece of data to the… to the ones who are asking for that particular piece of data. And so it's really more…

233
00:27:47.820 --> 00:27:48.280
Dominik Zajkowski: Oh, sorry.

234
00:27:48.280 --> 00:27:51.610
Kent Dybvig: It's… it's in the query, it's not in the… it's not in the…

235
00:27:52.100 --> 00:28:00.929
Kent Dybvig: Creation of the database entries, the database indexes, it's not in the… it's not any… anything about actually doing the log.

236
00:28:01.160 --> 00:28:02.760
Kent Dybvig: It's about querying it.

237
00:28:02.900 --> 00:28:05.060
Kent Dybvig: And I, yeah, I can see that for sure.

238
00:28:05.450 --> 00:28:21.089
Dominik Zajkowski: It's a hint for the indexer to properly store it, to surface it as an efficient indexed data point. Right, right. But that's technical, right? From a user's perspective, it doesn't really matter if we target indexed or not. The point is that the user wants to be able to…

239
00:28:21.380 --> 00:28:24.220
Dominik Zajkowski: Query for it, limit the amount of results.

240
00:28:24.580 --> 00:28:28.120
Dominik Zajkowski: Yet preserves their privacy as much as possible.

241
00:28:28.120 --> 00:28:33.999
Kent Dybvig: So we're basically telling… we basically have an educational problem. If we're going to provide this feature.

242
00:28:34.140 --> 00:28:35.500
Kent Dybvig: with an index.

243
00:28:35.710 --> 00:28:44.679
Kent Dybvig: we have an education problem. But the other alternative is to say, forget the indexing feature, we're not going to support it at all, because it's too dangerous.

244
00:28:45.190 --> 00:28:47.880
Kent Dybvig: Where do we think we should land on that?

245
00:28:51.250 --> 00:28:54.540
Dominik Zajkowski: That's a question to the community, I think.

246
00:28:59.930 --> 00:29:02.040
Dominik Zajkowski: the indexing problem…

247
00:29:02.290 --> 00:29:10.720
Dominik Zajkowski: over time becomes a burden. Well, the lack of indexing for data points becomes a massive burden.

248
00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:22.359
Dominik Zajkowski: So… From the implementation standpoint, it's a bit of work, sure, but it doesn't automatically disclose anything.

249
00:29:22.750 --> 00:29:23.850
Dominik Zajkowski: And…

250
00:29:23.950 --> 00:29:32.660
Dominik Zajkowski: Making it available doesn't mean that it will become used, so there is a risk of that feature, granted, for sure.

251
00:29:32.780 --> 00:29:38.500
Dominik Zajkowski: But if indexers do not surface this querying capability.

252
00:29:40.330 --> 00:29:48.199
Dominik Zajkowski: Or users never use it, then it becomes opt-in as a potential useful thing.

253
00:29:48.310 --> 00:29:51.199
Dominik Zajkowski: So… from a…

254
00:29:52.210 --> 00:30:00.790
Dominik Zajkowski: Trying to appease the community early point of view, and also creating an experience that's similar to what other chains are doing.

255
00:30:01.120 --> 00:30:04.249
Dominik Zajkowski: I think it's valuable, making it available.

256
00:30:04.500 --> 00:30:11.050
Dominik Zajkowski: And… Allowing for, essentially, people that use it to tell us if it's useful.

257
00:30:11.190 --> 00:30:19.810
Dominik Zajkowski: But I'm happy to circle back to the Foundation and ask them this question… this particular question to have… to get a very explicit response.

258
00:30:20.520 --> 00:30:27.040
Kent Dybvig: Yeah, I think… I think this is where it'd be useful to have Thomas' feedback, too, because this is a,

259
00:30:27.820 --> 00:30:34.359
Kent Dybvig: In legal terms, an attractive hazard, which means you've built something that, is dangerous.

260
00:30:34.630 --> 00:30:37.630
Kent Dybvig: And, you make it easy to use.

261
00:30:37.970 --> 00:30:44.980
Kent Dybvig: At some… at some point legally, or ethically at least, you're… you're liable for… for that use.

262
00:30:45.660 --> 00:30:47.120
Dominik Zajkowski: Understood. So we should be careful.

263
00:30:47.980 --> 00:30:59.769
Dominik Zajkowski: I'll make… well, this is part of the original request, so it was requested, but I will draw the picture better, so that the decision is made explicitly.

264
00:31:00.280 --> 00:31:00.870
Kent Dybvig: Okay.

265
00:31:02.950 --> 00:31:03.740
Kent Dybvig: Kevin?

266
00:31:06.690 --> 00:31:19.229
Kevin Millikin: Yeah, I had two questions. So, the proposal says that log is an expression, and I agree that it should be an expression, if only because then it can appear in the right-hand side of arrow functions.

267
00:31:19.230 --> 00:31:29.679
Kevin Millikin: But we have to say what the value of it is. It seems like the two possibilities are the empty tuple, or the… or the value of the subexpression that was logged.

268
00:31:31.640 --> 00:31:35.970
Dominik Zajkowski: I assumed empty, but this was just my assumption.

269
00:31:36.400 --> 00:31:46.329
Kevin Millikin: Yeah, it could be… it could be either one. They both seem to make sense. Assert is an expression like that, I think, that has a… has no value.

270
00:31:46.500 --> 00:31:47.100
Kevin Millikin: Yeah.

271
00:31:47.100 --> 00:31:50.799
Kent Dybvig: Ledger right is also that way, ledger increment.

272
00:31:51.820 --> 00:31:56.869
Kevin Millikin: So that seems to make sense. And then… but the other question is, is, have we thought about, what…

273
00:31:57.710 --> 00:32:16.959
Kevin Millikin: what the… what it means for logging to be correct. Like, if I log that I sent a coin, but I didn't do it, what… how does that go… what was wrong, there? And then the flip side is, if I have a contract that has a requirement that whenever I send a coin, I log it.

274
00:32:17.110 --> 00:32:26.520
Kevin Millikin: Somehow, because somebody wants to watch that, and I forget to do it. Does something go wrong, or is that just, oops, oops, I didn't log my coin spend?

275
00:32:29.610 --> 00:32:37.530
Dominik Zajkowski: I have two answers, as usual, because that's the beauty of doing it in stages. The current request is…

276
00:32:37.800 --> 00:32:47.250
Dominik Zajkowski: simplicity. Get it done quickly, deliver it. It pushes the burden of verification to the user.

277
00:32:47.390 --> 00:32:58.399
Dominik Zajkowski: Unfortunately, into user space. You have to understand your contract, you have to know what you're calling, and you have to make sure that what you're calling is actually the thing that you wanted to achieve, unfortunately.

278
00:32:58.770 --> 00:33:00.599
Dominik Zajkowski: Long term.

279
00:33:04.510 --> 00:33:08.800
Dominik Zajkowski: It's not obvious how to make sure that logging happened.

280
00:33:09.440 --> 00:33:14.460
Dominik Zajkowski: But it is a concern that needs… fought. So, yes.

281
00:33:14.570 --> 00:33:25.990
Dominik Zajkowski: Unfortunately, I don't have a good answer at the moment, but it is a concern that should be addressed. It's been captured in the follow-up work alongside private events.

282
00:33:26.180 --> 00:33:32.900
Dominik Zajkowski: Will there be will and need to pursue it? I don't know that, but we are aware.

283
00:33:37.920 --> 00:33:45.830
Kent Dybvig: I think that's a requirement, just like any other requirement to, that you might have to… to even store something in the ledger.

284
00:33:46.340 --> 00:33:48.529
Kent Dybvig: So… I think…

285
00:33:48.630 --> 00:33:55.289
Kent Dybvig: I don't think it's our job to ensure… certainly, I don't think it's our job to ensure that logging happens when it…

286
00:33:55.590 --> 00:33:57.740
Kent Dybvig: When it's supposed to, because that's…

287
00:33:58.120 --> 00:34:04.590
Kent Dybvig: That's a difficult thing to capture in… And… Without having some global…

288
00:34:04.690 --> 00:34:13.100
Kent Dybvig: Globally accessible declaration of contract behavior for some particular class of contracts, and we're nowhere near having anything like that.

289
00:34:13.699 --> 00:34:24.300
Kent Dybvig: saying that you didn't lie, well, I don't even… I don't even know how you do that. You'd have to really under… the compiler would have to understand all the events and…

290
00:34:24.520 --> 00:34:29.840
Kent Dybvig: And what it means to say something happened, and understand that

291
00:34:29.969 --> 00:34:33.670
Kent Dybvig: There was also a requirement to do some store in the ledger.

292
00:34:34.760 --> 00:34:47.530
Kevin Millikin: Yeah, I haven't thought about it, but what I was thinking is that, like, if you want to log that you sent a coin, that the actual sending of the coin gives you some kind of token that you could log.

293
00:34:47.699 --> 00:34:52.260
Kevin Millikin: That you couldn't cons that up your… you couldn't forge that, you couldn't build that yourself.

294
00:34:52.420 --> 00:34:57.390
Kevin Millikin: It would… it would only be actually spending the coin that would give you that thing that you could log.

295
00:35:00.990 --> 00:35:01.670
Kent Dybvig: M.

296
00:35:01.920 --> 00:35:06.539
Kevin Millikin: I haven't thought… I haven't thought clearly about, like, what that would entail, or how we could enforce that.

297
00:35:06.700 --> 00:35:07.280
Kent Dybvig: Right.

298
00:35:08.860 --> 00:35:09.440
Dominik Zajkowski: country?

299
00:35:10.890 --> 00:35:18.189
Andrzej Kopeć: So, two comments, regarding, the return type from the log expression.

300
00:35:18.660 --> 00:35:25.420
Andrzej Kopeć: I think it would make a lot of sense to actually return what was locked?

301
00:35:26.020 --> 00:35:36.849
Andrzej Kopeć: Specifically because in many, many cases, there might be some, circuit that was,

302
00:35:38.070 --> 00:35:46.499
Andrzej Kopeć: That was asked to prepare some data. It decides to log it, and it becomes the,

303
00:35:46.970 --> 00:35:54.820
Andrzej Kopeć: the return value immediately. So, the same thing that is being, that is being returned from the…

304
00:35:55.470 --> 00:36:01.899
Andrzej Kopeć: from the Kali, it's, it's also the same thing that's being logged, and this is…

305
00:36:02.110 --> 00:36:05.620
Andrzej Kopeć: In many ways similar to how

306
00:36:06.110 --> 00:36:11.479
Andrzej Kopeć: Notion of traces are, are often implemented in functional world.

307
00:36:11.810 --> 00:36:13.530
Andrzej Kopeć: Yeah.

308
00:36:13.530 --> 00:36:22.239
Kent Dybvig: Andre, I think I… I sympathize with that, but these are specifically exception records that we're creating.

309
00:36:22.890 --> 00:36:36.009
Kent Dybvig: They may have multiple values, fields… field… multiple fields with different values. Are you saying that it would be actually useful to have the exception value returned, or some member of the exception value?

310
00:36:40.970 --> 00:36:44.240
Andrzej Kopeć: What do you mean by exception value? I'm not sure if I follow.

311
00:36:48.000 --> 00:36:49.420
Andrzej Kopeć: Or… So…

312
00:36:49.420 --> 00:36:58.609
Kent Dybvig: So we have specific kinds of exceptions, each of which has multiple fields, like that. I'm sure LeBurn has a sender, amount, token type.

313
00:36:59.320 --> 00:37:04.040
Kent Dybvig: The event itself packages them up.

314
00:37:04.150 --> 00:37:06.750
Kent Dybvig: As… in a struct-like thing.

315
00:37:07.790 --> 00:37:11.730
Kent Dybvig: Really is a struct, but we also have this extra index information

316
00:37:12.150 --> 00:37:15.120
Kent Dybvig: And as far as compact is concerned.

317
00:37:20.560 --> 00:37:27.989
Kent Dybvig: You… do you really want… is it really useful to return the struct that… that may put… put together information that isn't…

318
00:37:28.200 --> 00:37:32.210
Kent Dybvig: isn't, isn't altogether needed.

319
00:37:34.190 --> 00:37:35.720
Andrzej Kopeć: So…

320
00:37:40.150 --> 00:37:41.740
Andrzej Kopeć: I find it useful.

321
00:37:42.100 --> 00:37:46.139
Andrzej Kopeć: And it is the way I tend to structure my own code.

322
00:37:47.150 --> 00:37:48.680
Andrzej Kopeć: A lot of the time.

323
00:37:49.410 --> 00:37:58.390
Andrzej Kopeć: To create a notion of a pipeline, and if there is any logging or something, it is, really often…

324
00:37:58.670 --> 00:38:01.880
Andrzej Kopeć: Performed either

325
00:38:02.930 --> 00:38:13.329
Andrzej Kopeć: At the very entry point to a particular function, let's call it that way, or at the very end, almost the same thing as the return value.

326
00:38:15.060 --> 00:38:21.940
Kent Dybvig: Yeah. No, I sympathize with it. I think it also addresses Evan's concern to a small extent, at least.

327
00:38:22.150 --> 00:38:26.090
Kent Dybvig: That, you know, you're logging something you're actually doing.

328
00:38:26.420 --> 00:38:33.940
Kent Dybvig: It helps you… But that structure helps you guarantee that you're doing the same thing you're logging.

329
00:38:36.080 --> 00:38:38.620
Andrzej Kopeć: And…

330
00:38:39.690 --> 00:38:45.120
Kent Dybvig: But it makes… it makes these exception records some… like, it promotes them into…

331
00:38:46.360 --> 00:38:55.630
Kent Dybvig: Packages, like, standard packages that one might use in their… in their programs for putting together things that are relevant for a particular purpose.

332
00:38:56.700 --> 00:39:00.410
Kent Dybvig: I don't know if they're all really… Set up that way.

333
00:39:08.110 --> 00:39:13.810
Dominik Zajkowski: I'm… Happy to put it, into the proposal, and essentially incorporate it there.

334
00:39:14.220 --> 00:39:21.249
Dominik Zajkowski: At current point, doesn't matter that much, because it's just a design. I guess it'll be ironed out in the Koip.

335
00:39:23.060 --> 00:39:25.379
Kent Dybvig: Yeah, I don't… I don't object to it, I think.

336
00:39:26.050 --> 00:39:28.570
Kent Dybvig: We didn't do it for… for…

337
00:39:28.780 --> 00:39:32.490
Kent Dybvig: For the increment and decrement operators.

338
00:39:32.700 --> 00:39:41.170
Kent Dybvig: Because… The obvious reason we don't… we don't want the, the incremented value to be…

339
00:39:41.750 --> 00:39:43.850
Kent Dybvig: To be part of the, transcript.

340
00:39:44.640 --> 00:39:49.330
Kent Dybvig: Here there's nothing that… that we're doing that's… that's gonna put this anywhere.

341
00:39:53.510 --> 00:39:59.650
Kevin Millikin: I think it does make sense that you just return the logged value, that the value of the logged expression is a sub-expression.

342
00:40:00.680 --> 00:40:01.260
Kevin Millikin: That seems.

343
00:40:01.260 --> 00:40:03.320
Kent Dybvig: Which is this… which is this struct.

344
00:40:03.480 --> 00:40:06.909
Kent Dybvig: There's one of these standard strokes in most cases. Okay, yes?

345
00:40:08.030 --> 00:40:10.699
Kevin Millikin: We didn't do that for a cert, because it would always be true.

346
00:40:12.550 --> 00:40:13.220
Kent Dybvig: Yes.

347
00:40:16.190 --> 00:40:18.450
Dominik Zajkowski: Can't you have your hand up?

348
00:40:20.160 --> 00:40:20.880
Kent Dybvig: Not anymore.

349
00:40:21.070 --> 00:40:22.270
Dominik Zajkowski: Not anymore. Okay.

350
00:40:22.710 --> 00:40:28.089
