00:42:01.240 --> 00:42:11.109
Kent Dybvig: So, Thomas, one thing… one issue that came up was whether we should support indexing, given that querying

366
00:42:11.250 --> 00:42:15.419
Kent Dybvig: With something more specific than saying, give me everything.

367
00:42:15.800 --> 00:42:23.379
Kent Dybvig: Can tend to identify, you know, can tend to tie you to a particular kind of data, tie a user to a particular,

368
00:42:23.510 --> 00:42:26.080
Kent Dybvig: Values of a particular data.

369
00:42:26.470 --> 00:42:32.440
Kent Dybvig: Do you think that's ample reason to not support indexing at all, or should we just…

370
00:42:32.590 --> 00:42:36.359
Kent Dybvig: Put it in there and educate people on the… on the risks.

371
00:42:46.030 --> 00:42:49.880
Kent Dybvig: So, as I understand it, the risk is that,

372
00:42:50.520 --> 00:42:57.710
Kent Dybvig: you could query down to some very specific thing, so your DAP on your behalf could query to some specific thing.

373
00:42:58.160 --> 00:43:05.869
Kent Dybvig: That identifies you as the person who sent a particular coin, or you that made a particular vote, or…

374
00:43:05.980 --> 00:43:07.329
Kent Dybvig: Or something else.

375
00:43:08.650 --> 00:43:26.070
Kent Dybvig: And so the… it's not the storing… it's not the logging that's dangerous, it's not the creating of the index is potentially dangerous, it's actually querying, with too precise of an index that limits the, set of people who are… who are possibly asking for this information.

376
00:43:29.870 --> 00:43:37.200
Dominik Zajkowski: And the education there would be that the indexers and any… essentially anything that is leveraging this indexing mechanism

377
00:43:37.720 --> 00:43:43.220
Dominik Zajkowski: Would have to… Prevent asking for too many…

378
00:43:45.430 --> 00:43:50.260
Dominik Zajkowski: Yeah, and just to be clear, it is possible to make those queries today.

379
00:43:51.860 --> 00:44:01.380
Dominik Zajkowski: We just don't create a… Mmm… easily Accessible data source.

380
00:44:03.250 --> 00:44:07.920
Dominik Zajkowski: So it is possible to go and index all nullifiers and then query for them.

381
00:44:08.030 --> 00:44:09.300
Dominik Zajkowski: Or commitments.

382
00:44:11.340 --> 00:44:13.410
Kent Dybvig: But we don't do it in the indexer.

383
00:44:13.950 --> 00:44:16.199
Kent Dybvig: And allowed the indexer to be queried.

384
00:44:17.860 --> 00:44:21.220
Dominik Zajkowski: This doesn't mean that it's not possible. Like, any, any, any…

385
00:44:21.840 --> 00:44:29.009
Dominik Zajkowski: The changes to the GraphQL structure are fairly trivial, so it is possible to just roll your own indexer and have.

386
00:44:29.010 --> 00:44:29.530
Kent Dybvig: Yeah.

387
00:44:29.530 --> 00:44:30.250
Dominik Zajkowski: data available.

388
00:44:30.250 --> 00:44:30.870
Kent Dybvig: For sure.

389
00:44:31.190 --> 00:44:37.400
Dominik Zajkowski: publish it, make it into a better midnight tool, and then start gathering that information.

390
00:44:37.780 --> 00:44:38.550
Kent Dybvig: Yeah.

391
00:44:38.690 --> 00:44:41.090
Dominik Zajkowski: I just disclosed the attack vector.

392
00:44:49.880 --> 00:44:50.580
Kent Dybvig: Andre?

393
00:44:50.580 --> 00:44:59.359
Andrzej Kopeć: So… in many ways, the responsibilities on a DApp developer who really wants

394
00:44:59.580 --> 00:45:05.020
Andrzej Kopeć: To care about privacy are more or less the same as on the wallet developer.

395
00:45:05.230 --> 00:45:13.909
Andrzej Kopeć: And, the understanding of what happens, what might get disclosed at what point.

396
00:45:14.660 --> 00:45:17.090
Andrzej Kopeć: They're kind of there all the time.

397
00:45:17.610 --> 00:45:20.850
Andrzej Kopeć: So, so I agree with, with Thomas that…

398
00:45:21.280 --> 00:45:30.910
Andrzej Kopeć: in case any API on the indexer is exposed that allows to narrow down certain values, with commitments and nullifiers being,

399
00:45:31.160 --> 00:45:39.380
Andrzej Kopeć: the particularly sensitive, examples in that area, then they need to… Then…

400
00:45:39.940 --> 00:45:44.039
Andrzej Kopeć: A piece of education is needed, how to use it.

401
00:45:44.200 --> 00:45:48.900
Andrzej Kopeć: And that the right API structure should be exposed from the indexer.

402
00:45:49.070 --> 00:45:51.779
Andrzej Kopeć: To… to help to build the right thing.

403
00:45:53.200 --> 00:45:57.609
Andrzej Kopeć: But, actually, I think there is…

404
00:45:58.050 --> 00:46:06.740
Andrzej Kopeć: There might be, or there might be not. I honestly don't know. A purely technical problem, related to…

405
00:46:07.030 --> 00:46:13.119
Andrzej Kopeć: encoding the data, or enabling the indexer to reasonably index the data, that is…

406
00:46:13.740 --> 00:46:21.429
Andrzej Kopeć: The state value is all… Filter-lined binary encoded.

407
00:46:22.400 --> 00:46:34.020
Andrzej Kopeć: Which… Makes me wonder… Is it a format that, on its own, would be…

408
00:46:34.190 --> 00:46:38.489
Andrzej Kopeć: reasonable to… to process by the indexer, or…

409
00:46:38.660 --> 00:46:45.729
Andrzej Kopeć: Would such event require actual extensions that help to parse it meaningfully?

410
00:46:46.760 --> 00:46:51.319
Kent Dybvig: Well, I think the indexer would have to be in on the schema for these things, which is…

411
00:46:51.740 --> 00:46:54.550
Kent Dybvig: Standard, at least for the standard events.

412
00:46:56.550 --> 00:47:04.100
Dominik Zajkowski: That's one approach, and that's the intended use case for the standard events, just that that's why they exist.

413
00:47:04.260 --> 00:47:10.420
Dominik Zajkowski: But also, there is a mechanism that I leveraged, and it might be…

414
00:47:10.570 --> 00:47:15.209
Dominik Zajkowski: Too much information about, data being passed, but…

415
00:47:15.370 --> 00:47:26.019
Dominik Zajkowski: As far as I understand it, alongside the binary data, there is an alignment data that is passed around, and it's a different representation, it's based around atoms.

416
00:47:26.500 --> 00:47:31.240
Dominik Zajkowski: But those atoms have sizes, and they can be indexed.

417
00:47:31.510 --> 00:47:37.640
Dominik Zajkowski: So, as long as we're able to translate a field name into that atom index.

418
00:47:38.630 --> 00:47:47.090
Dominik Zajkowski: It is possible to convey this information in a way that the index, without knowing the structure itself, will be able to index the right

419
00:47:47.720 --> 00:47:48.630
Dominik Zajkowski: blob.

420
00:47:49.500 --> 00:47:55.069
Andrzej Kopeć: But… the… this is exactly where I see a potential issue.

421
00:47:55.930 --> 00:47:58.190
Andrzej Kopeć: That is…

422
00:47:59.370 --> 00:48:10.370
Andrzej Kopeć: the atoms, they might be very deeply nested. It really depends on the exact structure of the data being emitted, and if there is, like, I don't know.

423
00:48:10.610 --> 00:48:19.729
Andrzej Kopeć: three layers of nesting structs, then it will be at least 3 layers of nesting arrays in the… in the alignment.

424
00:48:20.380 --> 00:48:22.530
Andrzej Kopeć: No, I'm…

425
00:48:22.530 --> 00:48:23.380
Dominik Zajkowski: is flat.

426
00:48:28.090 --> 00:48:34.490
Kent Dybvig: But it's a flattened version of a nested structure, and if you're trying to pull out the third field.

427
00:48:35.540 --> 00:48:39.060
Kent Dybvig: Requires a bit of work to identify where the first two.

428
00:48:39.060 --> 00:48:39.720
Dominik Zajkowski: Yes.

429
00:48:40.370 --> 00:48:40.970
Dominik Zajkowski: That's true.

430
00:48:41.400 --> 00:48:44.360
Kent Dybvig: Where the flattened version of each of the first two ends.

431
00:48:47.050 --> 00:48:48.790
Dominik Zajkowski: Agreed, it's not trivial.

432
00:48:48.930 --> 00:49:04.879
Dominik Zajkowski: My point is that it is potentially doable in a default events, not existing scenario, or not being utilized. But that's an open question if we want to support that or not.

433
00:49:14.010 --> 00:49:20.159
Kent Dybvig: I had one other question that may or may not be in the scope of this, which is, how long do these

434
00:49:22.230 --> 00:49:25.150
Kent Dybvig: Events live in…

435
00:49:25.260 --> 00:49:37.560
Kent Dybvig: in the database, these aren't, like, ledger entries, which are permanent, which need to be permanent, which are needed for replay on the blockchain. These are things that ADAP will use

436
00:49:38.080 --> 00:49:40.960
Kent Dybvig: In real time, effectively.

437
00:49:41.280 --> 00:49:44.590
Kent Dybvig: To… to scan for things that happened.

438
00:49:45.000 --> 00:49:51.830
Kent Dybvig: Is it… is it… is there a timeout on these things? Can we set a… set a limit so that they… so the database doesn't become clogged?

439
00:49:52.860 --> 00:49:55.290
Kent Dybvig: Or, you know, go grow without bound.

440
00:49:55.770 --> 00:49:58.930
Dominik Zajkowski: You mean the database in the indexer, or on the node?

441
00:49:58.930 --> 00:49:59.540
Kent Dybvig: Yeah.

442
00:50:00.210 --> 00:50:00.890
Dominik Zajkowski: Oh, index.

443
00:50:00.890 --> 00:50:01.460
Kent Dybvig: indexer.

444
00:50:01.460 --> 00:50:05.830
Dominik Zajkowski: That's up to policy. That's up to… per item, per use case.

445
00:50:06.110 --> 00:50:07.299
Dominik Zajkowski: I think it's for dimension.

446
00:50:07.300 --> 00:50:11.700
Kent Dybvig: I think it's worth mentioning that these things don't necessarily live forever.

447
00:50:11.960 --> 00:50:16.419
Dominik Zajkowski: Understood. Yeah, that's fair, that's fair. But I don't want to be prescriptive.

448
00:50:16.750 --> 00:50:24.439
Dominik Zajkowski: Because, at the end of the day, I could imagine… A mechanism which, well.

449
00:50:25.800 --> 00:50:31.930
Dominik Zajkowski: let's say DEX, which gathers all of this information and wants to have access to all of them all the time.

